  "question_origin": "researcher_constructed",
  "question_text": "Tavora and Belora are trading partners with flexible exchange rates. Tavora uses the crown and Belora uses the peso. Initially Tavora has a zero current-account balance and a zero capital-and-financial-account balance. Assume no change in net investment income, transfers, official reserve transactions, or statistical discrepancies. Real income in Tavora now increases; hold other determinants of trade constant. Assume that exchange-rate feedback does not fully offset the initial effect of the income change on trade flows.\n\n(a) What happens to Tavora’s net exports? Explain.\n\n(b) Draw a correctly labeled foreign-exchange graph for the peso, with crowns per peso on the vertical axis. Show the effect of the change in part (a) on the demand for pesos and on the peso’s international value.\n\n(c) What happens to Tavora’s capital-and-financial-account balance? Explain using the balance-of-payments identity.\n\n(d) Based solely on the increase in Belora’s exports, what happens to cyclical unemployment in Belora in the short run? Explain, assuming it initially has a recessionary gap.",
