      "answer": {
        "answer_markdown": "**(a)** Tavora’s net exports decrease. Higher real income raises Tavora’s demand for imports, including goods from Belora. With other trade determinants held constant, imports rise while exports are initially unchanged, so net exports (exports minus imports) fall. The stated exchange-rate assumption means this effect is not fully offset.\n\n**(b)** Tavora’s increased imports from Belora increase Tavora’s demand for pesos: Tavora’s residents need pesos to buy Beloran goods. The peso demand curve shifts right, raising its value in crowns per peso (the peso appreciates).\n\n**(c)** Tavora’s capital-and-financial-account balance increases (moves toward surplus). With the current account lower and the initial balance-of-payments accounts at zero, the balance-of-payments identity requires an offsetting increase in the capital-and-financial-account balance. Given the assumptions, the decrease in the current-account balance is matched by an increase in the capital-and-financial-account balance.\n\n**(d)** Belora’s cyclical unemployment decreases in the short run. Higher exports increase aggregate demand and output; with Belora initially in a recessionary gap, the rise in production increases demand for labor and reduces cyclical unemployment.",
